void *a = calloc(1, 0x1000);

int *b = a;

printf("%x\n",*b++)
	is fine postfix is higher priority than * so it will do the right thing

but
 (int*)a++  is not. Well it will increment postfix but by 1 not sizeof(int) because of the same priority reason


Now your next instinct will be to do this
 ((int*)a)++  
  heck braces beat all right? sure but output of a Cast is *NOT* a l-value 
  http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/bkbs2cds.aspx (apparently MS has an extension that allows this but ANSI C doesnt)

so cant place that expression on the L side ... :) and   Exp++ assumes Exp is an l-value


===== MORAL ===========
   postfix / unary operators operate only on modifiable lvalues.   output of a cast is not a modifiable lvalue therefore
   cannot be used with these operators OR used on the l side of an assignment operation.

